8. SAMPLE SQL OPERATIONS

The following SQL operations were performed in the project.

Display all students:

sql
SELECT * FROM Students;


Display all books:

sql
SELECT * FROM Books;


Display available books:

sql
SELECT *
FROM Books
WHERE Available_Quantity > 0;


Display books issued to a particular student:

sql
SELECT
    S.Student_Name,
    B.Title,
    B.Author,
    I.Issue_Date,
    I.Status
FROM Book_Issue I
INNER JOIN Students S
ON I.Student_ID = S.Student_ID
INNER JOIN Books B
ON I.Book_ID = B.Book_ID
WHERE S.Student_Name = 'Rahul Sharma';


Count total students:

sql
SELECT COUNT(*) AS Total_Students
FROM Students;


Calculate total books:

sql
SELECT SUM(Quantity) AS Total_Books
FROM Books;

9. WEBSITE CONNECTIVITY

The Library Management System database can be connected to a website using a backend programming language such as PHP, Python, or Java.

The frontend can be developed using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The backend connects the website to the MySQL database through a database connector.

When a student searches for a book, the backend executes an SQL query and retrieves the required information from the database. The results are then displayed on the website.

This allows students and librarians to access library information through a user-friendly interface.

10. DISTRIBUTED DATABASE APPLICATION

A distributed database can support libraries located at different campus locations.

For example, a university may have libraries at its Main Campus, Engineering Campus, and Medical Campus.

Each library can maintain its own book and issue records while sharing information with other campus libraries.

Advantages include:

Students can search for books across different campuses.
Libraries can share book availability information.
Each campus can manage its own records.
Data replication can improve availability.
Students can access library information from different locations.

The current project uses a single MySQL database, but it can be expanded into a distributed database system in the future.

